{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-176623",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We need to isolate the variable $x.$ First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+1}{3} & =\\frac{y-x}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot (2x+1) & =3\\cdot (y-x) \\\\ 4x+2 & =3y-3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $2$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2 & =3y-3x \\\\ 4x+2-2 & =3y-3x-2 \\\\ 4x & =3y-3x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $3x$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =3y-3x-2 \\\\ 4x+3x & =3y-3x-2+3x \\\\ 7x & =3y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x & =3y-2 \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & =\\frac{3y-2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7}x}{\\cancel{7}} & =\\frac{3y-2}{7} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3y-2}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
